#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""spatium-k3s-images — every image the slot bakes is in containerd, or it says so (#1630).

The appliance ships its container images as tarballs in
/var/lib/rancher/k3s/agent/images/, and k3s imports them into containerd
(k3s pkg/agent/containerd/watcher.go). It imports a tarball when the file is
new to it or its size or mtime changed, which a slot upgrade does to every
tarball it copies across. Every pod on the appliance runs with
``imagePullPolicy: Never``, so a tarball that did not import is a workload
that never starts: ``ErrImageNeverPull``.

#1630: a slot upgrade's first boot failed to import seven tarballs, the DNS
and DHCP agents' among them, and nothing noticed:

  failed to ingest "index.json": failed commit on ref "tar-index.json":
  commit failed: unexpected commit size 3071, expected 374

  * k3s imports each tar member under the containerd ref ``tar-<name>``
    (``archive.ImportIndex``), so every tarball's ``index.json`` shares the
    ref ``tar-index.json`` in the ``k8s.io`` namespace.
  * A write under a ref that is not committed stays on disk as an "ingest".
    It does so when the import reading it fails part-way or is killed.
  * containerd resumes an ingest whose recorded size matches. Otherwise it
    starts it over, but reopens the old data file without truncating it
    (containerd plugins/content/local/store.go ``writer``). A shorter blob
    then fails its commit on the file's old length (writer.go ``Commit``).
  * So one stale ``index.json`` fails every shorter ``index.json`` imported
    after it, until a longer one commits over it. It survives restarts.
  * k3s records only the tarballs that imported (``.cache.json``) and does
    not retry a failed one until the file changes again.

Three subcommands:

  clear-ingests   remove containerd's unfinished content writes. k3s.service
                  runs it as an ExecStartPre: no import can be in flight
                  then, because k3s's containerd dies with the k3s process
                  that started it (k3s containerd.go ``addDeathSig``).
                  containerd's own ``Abort(ref)`` is exactly this removal
                  (store.go ``Abort``). The metadata entry left behind is
                  harmless: the next write under that ref finds nothing to
                  resume and starts clean.

  verify          once k3s's start-up import is over, check that k3s
                  imported every baked tarball (its own record,
                  ``.cache.json``) and that every image those tarballs carry
                  is in containerd. With ``--repair``, clear the ingests no
                  import is writing, and make k3s re-import what it missed
                  by moving the tarball's mtime (the watcher re-imports a
                  file whose mtime moved past its cache entry). Exit 0 when
                  everything is there, 1 when something is still missing
                  or still being imported when ``--max-wait`` runs out.
                  spatiumddi-firstboot runs it before it commits a trial
                  slot.

  unpack          once k3s's start-up import is over, unpack the imported
                  images this node's workloads will run into containerd's
                  snapshotter, one at a time and paced, so that the
                  kubelet finds them unpacked when it starts them.
                  spatiumddi-image-unpack.service runs it on every boot.
                  Best effort: whatever it leaves, the kubelet unpacks.

#2788: k3s's import writes an image into containerd's content store and no
further. containerd unpacks its layers into the snapshotter when the kubelet
creates the image's first container. A rolling upgrade keeps every node on
the old images until the last node is done, then moves the shared image tags
in one step (#1710), so every node unpacked its new images at once, 1-2 GB
each, on the /var that etcd writes its WAL to. On a shared disk that held
etcd's fdatasync for seconds, a kube-scheduler lease ran out, and k3s exited
in the middle of the node's DNS and DHCP pod swap. Unpacking after the
node's reboot moves those writes into its own turn, one node at a time, and
paces them like the upgrade's other writes on /var (#2090).

#2109: "is k3s still importing?" is answered by whether a process holds a
baked tarball open. k3s opens the file for the whole import and closes it
when the import is done (containerd.go ``preloadFile``: wharfie's
``tarfile.GetOpener`` ``os.Open``s it under the zstd reader, closed by a
``defer`` after the images are labelled), so ``/proc/<pid>/fd`` names the
tarball exactly while it is being imported. The kubelet is NOT that signal:
k3s's start-up loop returns once its work queue is EMPTY, which is when the
last tarball is taken off it, not when that tarball's import ends, so the
kubelet starts with the last import still running. And on slow storage the
kubelet does not answer for minutes; judging then counted every tarball
k3s had not reached yet as missing and touched the one it was importing,
which restarted that import and lost the slot its commit.

Paths are env-overridable (``SPATIUM_K3S_IMAGES_DIR``,
``SPATIUM_K3S_CONTENT_DIR``, ``SPATIUM_K3S_BIN``, ``SPATIUM_K3S_PROC_DIR``,
``SPATIUM_K3S_KUBELET_HEALTHZ``) for the tests in
appliance/tests/test_k3s_images.py.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import json
import math
import os
import shutil
import subprocess
import sys
import time
import urllib.request
from dataclasses import dataclass, field
from datetime import datetime
from pathlib import Path

IMAGES_DIR = Path(os.environ.get("SPATIUM_K3S_IMAGES_DIR", "/var/lib/rancher/k3s/agent/images"))
CONTENT_DIR = Path(
    os.environ.get(
        "SPATIUM_K3S_CONTENT_DIR",
        "/var/lib/rancher/k3s/agent/containerd/io.containerd.content.v1.content",
    )
)
K3S = os.environ.get("SPATIUM_K3S_BIN", "/usr/local/bin/k3s")
PROC_DIR = Path(os.environ.get("SPATIUM_K3S_PROC_DIR", "/proc"))
# What k3s bakes and spatium-upgrade-slot copies across a slot upgrade.
TARBALL_GLOB = "*.tar.zst"
# The kubelet's healthz: k3s binds it to 127.0.0.1, or ::1 on a node with an
# IPv6 node IP (k3s pkg/daemons/agent/agent.go), on the kubelet's default port.
KUBELET_HEALTHZ = tuple(
    os.environ.get(
        "SPATIUM_K3S_KUBELET_HEALTHZ",
        "http://127.0.0.1:10248/healthz,http://[::1]:10248/healthz",
    ).split(",")
)
# An ingest none of whose files changed for this long has no import writing
# it. containerd touches the data file on every write.
IDLE_INGEST_S = 60.0
POLL_S = 5.0
# With the kubelet not answering, k3s counts as idle only once no baked
# tarball has been open for this long. Its queue moves from one tarball to
# the next in well under a second, and a file event it queues waits 2 s
# (watcher.go ``AddAfter``), so this outlasts every gap between two imports.
SETTLE_S = 15.0
PREFIX = "spatium-k3s-images:"
# #2788: the snapshotter the kubelet's containers unpack into. It is k3s's
# default, as the appliance's k3s config names none (test_k3s_images.py
# holds it to that).
SNAPSHOTTER = "overlayfs"
# The rate the upgrade's other writes on /var keep to since #2090
# (spatium-upgrade-slot ``_WRITE_RATE_BYTES_PER_SECOND``).
UNPACK_RATE_MIB_S = 32.0
# Below this share of /var free, leave the rest to the kubelet: its image GC
# starts at 95% used and it evicts pods below 5% free (k3s config.yaml).
MIN_FREE_SHARE = 0.10
MIB = 1024 * 1024


def say(msg: str, *, err: bool = False) -> None:
    print(f"{PREFIX} {msg}", file=sys.stderr if err else sys.stdout, flush=True)


def _sleep(seconds: float) -> None:
    time.sleep(seconds)


# ── stale ingests ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────


def _newest_mtime(d: Path) -> float:
    newest = d.stat().st_mtime
    for p in d.iterdir():
        try:
            newest = max(newest, p.stat().st_mtime)
        except OSError:
            # containerd may finish (rename away) a write between iterdir and
            # stat; a vanished file is not part of the ingest any more.
            continue
    return newest


def clear_ingests(min_idle_s: float = 0.0, now: float | None = None) -> list[tuple[str, int]]:
    """Remove containerd's unfinished content writes, the ones idle for at
    least ``min_idle_s`` (0: all of them). Returns ``(ref, bytes written)``
    for each one removed."""
    try:
        entries = sorted(p for p in (CONTENT_DIR / "ingest").iterdir() if p.is_dir())
    except OSError:
        return []
    now = time.time() if now is None else now
    cleared: list[tuple[str, int]] = []
    for d in entries:
        try:
            if min_idle_s and now - _newest_mtime(d) < min_idle_s:
                continue
            ref_file, data = d / "ref", d / "data"
            ref = ref_file.read_text(errors="replace").strip() if ref_file.exists() else d.name
            size = data.stat().st_size if data.exists() else 0
        except OSError:
            continue
        shutil.rmtree(d, ignore_errors=True)
        if not d.exists():
            cleared.append((ref, size))
    return cleared


# ── what k3s imported, and what containerd holds ─────────────────────────────


def read_import_cache() -> dict[str, dict]:
    """k3s's record of the tarballs it imported, keyed by file name:
    ``{"size", "modTime", "images"}`` (watcher.go ``fileInfo``; k3s keys it
    by full path). A tarball whose import failed has no entry."""
    try:
        raw = (IMAGES_DIR / ".cache.json").read_text()
        data = json.loads(raw) if raw.strip() else {}
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return {}
    if not isinstance(data, dict):
        return {}
    return {Path(k).name: v for k, v in data.items() if isinstance(v, dict)}


def _epoch(stamp: object) -> float | None:
    if not isinstance(stamp, str) or not stamp:
        return None
    try:
        return datetime.fromisoformat(stamp.replace("Z", "+00:00")).timestamp()
    except ValueError:
        return None


def imported(tarball: Path, entry: dict | None) -> bool:
    """Did k3s import this file as it is now? Its entry must carry the
    file's size, a modTime the file's mtime does not pass, and an image
    list. watcher.go re-imports when any of those is off (``file.ModTime().
    After(cached.ModTime)``) and writes the entry only after an import
    succeeded. k3s stores modTime to the second, so a file whose mtime has a
    fraction is After its own entry and is re-imported on every start: the
    comparison is exact, with no flooring (#2387)."""
    if not entry or not isinstance(entry.get("images"), list):
        return False
    try:
        st = tarball.stat()
    except OSError:
        return False
    if entry.get("size") != st.st_size:
        return False
    cached = _epoch(entry.get("modTime"))
    return cached is not None and st.st_mtime_ns <= round(cached * 1_000_000_000)


def present_images() -> set[str] | None:
    """The image names in containerd's ``k8s.io`` namespace, or None when
    containerd does not answer."""
    try:
        proc = subprocess.run(
            [K3S, "ctr", "-n", "k8s.io", "images", "ls", "-q"],
            capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=60, check=False,
        )
    except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired):
        return None
    if proc.returncode != 0:
        return None
    return {line.strip() for line in proc.stdout.splitlines() if line.strip()}


def kubelet_up() -> bool:
    for url in KUBELET_HEALTHZ:
        try:
            with urllib.request.urlopen(url, timeout=3) as resp:
                if resp.status == 200:
                    return True
        except (OSError, ValueError):
            continue
    return False


def importing(tarballs: list[Path]) -> set[Path]:
    """The tarballs some process holds open right now: the ones k3s is
    importing (#2109). Reads every ``/proc/<pid>/fd``, which needs root, as
    firstboot is. An fd that cannot be read counts as not open, so without
    the access this answers "nothing" and verify judges as it did before.

    An fd is matched by its file's (device, inode), not by the path its link
    reads: the kernel spells that path through the mount the opener used, so
    a bind mount or a symlinked directory gives the same file another name.
    Only links naming a ``*.tar.zst`` are stat'd, which keeps one scan to a
    readlink per fd on the host (a few thousand, a few milliseconds)."""
    by_id: dict[tuple[int, int], Path] = {}
    for t in tarballs:
        try:
            st = os.stat(t)
        except OSError:
            continue
        by_id[(st.st_dev, st.st_ino)] = t
    busy: set[Path] = set()
    if not by_id:
        return busy
    suffix = TARBALL_GLOB.lstrip("*")
    try:
        pids = [p for p in PROC_DIR.iterdir() if p.name.isdigit()]
    except OSError:
        return busy
    for pid in pids:
        try:
            fds = list(os.scandir(pid / "fd"))
        except OSError:
            # the process exited, or its fds are not ours to read
            continue
        for fd in fds:
            try:
                if not os.readlink(fd.path).endswith(suffix):
                    continue
                # stat through the fd: the open file itself, whatever its name
                st = os.stat(fd.path)
            except OSError:
                # the process closed it, or exited, between the two calls
                continue
            hit = by_id.get((st.st_dev, st.st_ino))
            if hit is not None:
                busy.add(hit)
    return busy


@dataclass
class Report:
    tarballs: list[Path]
    unimported: list[Path]          # k3s has no record of importing these
    missing: dict[str, list[str]]   # tarball name -> its images containerd lacks
    images: int                     # images the imported tarballs carry
    store: bool                     # containerd answered
    busy: set[Path] = field(default_factory=set)  # k3s is importing these now

    @property
    def ok(self) -> bool:
        return self.store and not self.unimported and not self.missing

    def names(self) -> list[str]:
        return [t.name for t in self.unimported] + [
            img for imgs in self.missing.values() for img in imgs
        ]


def inspect(busy: set[Path] | None = None) -> Report:
    cache = read_import_cache()
    tarballs = sorted(IMAGES_DIR.glob(TARBALL_GLOB))
    unimported = [t for t in tarballs if not imported(t, cache.get(t.name))]
    store = present_images()
    missing: dict[str, list[str]] = {}
    count = 0
    for t in tarballs:
        if t in unimported:
            continue
        names = [n for n in cache[t.name]["images"] if isinstance(n, str)]
        count += len(names)
        if store is not None:
            gone = [n for n in names if n not in store]
            if gone:
                missing[t.name] = gone
    return Report(tarballs, unimported, missing, count, store is not None, set(busy or ()))


def _describe(report: Report) -> None:
    if not report.store:
        say("containerd did not answer `k3s ctr images ls`", err=True)
    for t in report.unimported:
        if t in report.busy:
            say(f"k3s is still importing {t.name}; its images are not checked yet", err=True)
        else:
            say(f"k3s has not imported {t.name}", err=True)
    for name, imgs in report.missing.items():
        for img in imgs:
            say(f"{img} (from {name}) is not in containerd", err=True)


def _all_there(report: Report) -> None:
    say(f"all {len(report.tarballs)} baked tarballs imported, {report.images} images present")


# ── #2788: the slot's new images, unpacked in this node's own turn ───────────


def _ctr(*args: str, timeout: float = 60.0) -> subprocess.CompletedProcess | None:
    try:
        return subprocess.run(
            [K3S, "ctr", "-n", "k8s.io", *args],
            capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=timeout, check=False,
        )
    except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired):
        return None


def unpacked_images() -> set[str] | None:
    """The image names containerd holds whole and unpacked in the
    snapshotter, or None when it does not answer."""
    proc = _ctr("images", "check", "-q", "--snapshotter", SNAPSHOTTER)
    if proc is None or proc.returncode != 0:
        return None
    return {line.strip() for line in proc.stdout.splitlines() if line.strip()}


def _repository(name: str) -> str | None:
    """An image name without its tag or digest. None for an image ID
    (``sha256:...``), which names no repository."""
    name = name.split("@", 1)[0]
    slash = name.rfind("/")
    if slash < 0:
        return None
    colon = name.rfind(":")
    return name[:colon] if colon > slash else name


def to_unpack() -> list[str] | None:
    """The images k3s imported from the baked tarballs that are not unpacked
    yet, of a repository this node has an unpacked image of: the new
    release of a workload this node runs. The kubelet unpacks only the
    images it runs, so these are the ones the tag move would have it unpack
    on this node. None when containerd does not answer."""
    cache = read_import_cache()
    names: list[str] = []
    for t in sorted(IMAGES_DIR.glob(TARBALL_GLOB)):
        entry = cache.get(t.name)
        if imported(t, entry):
            names += [n for n in entry["images"] if isinstance(n, str)]
    present, ready = present_images(), unpacked_images()
    if present is None or ready is None:
        return None
    ran_here = {_repository(n) for n in ready} - {None}
    return [n for n in dict.fromkeys(names)
            if n in present and n not in ready and _repository(n) in ran_here]


def disk_usage() -> tuple[int, float] | None:
    """Bytes in use on containerd's filesystem (/var, where etcd's WAL is
    too) and the share of it still free, or None when it cannot be read."""
    try:
        st = os.statvfs(CONTENT_DIR.parent)
    except OSError:
        return None
    if not st.f_blocks:
        return None
    return (st.f_blocks - st.f_bfree) * st.f_frsize, st.f_bavail / st.f_blocks


def image_targets() -> dict[str, str] | None:
    """Each image name's target digest, which is how ``ctr snapshots
    unpack`` names an image, or None when containerd does not answer.
    ``ctr images ls`` prints REF, TYPE, DIGEST, then the rest."""
    proc = _ctr("images", "ls")
    if proc is None or proc.returncode != 0:
        return None
    targets: dict[str, str] = {}
    for line in proc.stdout.splitlines():
        cols = line.split()
        if len(cols) >= 3 and cols[2].startswith("sha256:"):
            targets[cols[0]] = cols[2]
    return targets


def unpack_image(ref: str, target: str, deadline: float) -> bool:
    """Unpack one image into the snapshotter, as the kubelet's first
    container from it would. ``ctr snapshots unpack`` runs containerd's own
    image unpack and nothing more: the layers stay unpacked, held by the
    image, and nothing else is left behind. It leaves the image's labels
    alone, k3s's ``io.cri-containerd.pinned`` among them, which keeps the
    kubelet's image GC off the rollback slot's images. Re-importing the
    tarball would unpack it too, but replaces those labels.

    Not ``ctr images mount``, which unpacks the same way and then mounts
    the image: on containerd 2.3 the mount is also activated under its
    target's name, and ``ctr images unmount`` never deactivates it, so the
    next image mounted there fails "already exists" until containerd's
    garbage collector has removed the old one. A ctr killed at the
    deadline leaves its half-unpacked layer under the 24-hour lease
    containerd's unpack takes, and the garbage collector removes it then."""
    timeout = max(deadline - time.monotonic(), 1.0)
    proc = _ctr("snapshots", "unpack", "--sync-fs", "--snapshotter", SNAPSHOTTER,
                target, timeout=timeout)
    if proc is not None and proc.returncode == 0:
        return True
    if proc is None:
        why = "containerd did not answer"
    else:
        lines = (proc.stderr or proc.stdout).strip().splitlines()
        why = lines[-1] if lines else f"exit {proc.returncode}"
    say(f"could not unpack {ref}: {why}", err=True)
    return False


def wait_for_containerd(deadline: float) -> bool:
    """Wait until containerd answers. k3s's containerd exits with k3s and
    comes back with the k3s that systemd restarts."""
    while present_images() is None:
        if time.monotonic() >= deadline:
            return False
        _sleep(POLL_S)
    return True


# ── commands ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────


def cmd_clear_ingests(args: argparse.Namespace) -> int:
    for ref, size in clear_ingests(min_idle_s=args.min_idle):
        say(f"cleared stale containerd ingest {ref} ({size} bytes)")
    return 0


def _names(tarballs: set[Path] | list[Path]) -> str:
    return ", ".join(sorted(t.name for t in tarballs))


def wait_for_import(kubelet_wait: float, hard: float) -> set[Path]:
    """Wait until k3s's start-up import is over (#2109). Over means no baked
    tarball is open AND either the kubelet answers (the fast path, no wait at
    all on a node that finished long ago) or, past ``kubelet_wait`` without
    one, nothing has been open for ``SETTLE_S``. While a tarball is open,
    keep waiting, up to the monotonic deadline ``hard``. Returns the
    tarballs still open when it stopped: empty unless ``hard`` ran out."""
    tarballs = sorted(IMAGES_DIR.glob(TARBALL_GLOB))
    started = time.monotonic()
    quiet_since: float | None = None
    told: set[Path] = set()
    while True:
        busy = importing(tarballs)
        now = time.monotonic()
        if busy:
            quiet_since = None
            if busy - told:
                say(f"k3s is importing {_names(busy)}; waiting for it to finish")
                told |= busy
        else:
            if kubelet_up():
                return set()
            if quiet_since is None:
                quiet_since = now
            if now >= kubelet_wait and now - quiet_since >= SETTLE_S:
                say(f"the kubelet did not answer within {kubelet_wait - started:g}s and k3s has "
                    "no image tarball open; judging k3s's import as it stands", err=True)
                return set()
        if now >= hard:
            if busy:
                say(f"k3s is still importing {_names(busy)} after {now - started:.0f}s; "
                    "out of time to wait for it", err=True)
            else:
                say(f"the kubelet did not answer within {now - started:.0f}s; "
                    "judging k3s's import as it stands", err=True)
            return busy
        _sleep(POLL_S)


def cmd_verify(args: argparse.Namespace) -> int:
    start = time.monotonic()
    hard = start + max(args.max_wait, 0.0)
    busy = wait_for_import(min(start + args.wait, hard), hard)
    report = inspect(busy)
    if report.ok:
        _all_there(report)
        return 0
    _describe(report)
    if not args.repair or not report.store:
        return 1

    for ref, size in clear_ingests(min_idle_s=IDLE_INGEST_S):
        say(f"cleared stale containerd ingest {ref} ({size} bytes)")
    wanted = set(report.unimported) | {IMAGES_DIR / name for name in report.missing}
    # #2109 — never touch a tarball k3s has open: moving its mtime mid-import
    # makes the watcher import it all over again once this import ends.
    busy = importing(sorted(wanted))
    if busy:
        say(f"leaving {_names(busy)} alone: k3s is importing it right now")
    redo = sorted(wanted - busy)
    if redo:
        say(f"re-importing {len(redo)} tarball(s) through k3s: {_names(redo)}")
    # A whole second: k3s keeps modTime to the second and re-imports a file
    # whose mtime is After it, so a fractional stamp is re-imported on every
    # boot, never settling (#2387). The ceiling keeps it past the old entry.
    now = math.ceil(time.time())
    for t in redo:
        os.utime(t, ns=(now * 1_000_000_000, now * 1_000_000_000))

    # Keep waiting past --repair-wait while k3s is importing (it may reach the
    # touched tarballs only after the ones ahead of them), never past --max-wait.
    deadline = min(time.monotonic() + args.repair_wait, hard)
    while True:
        _sleep(POLL_S)
        busy = importing(sorted(IMAGES_DIR.glob(TARBALL_GLOB)))
        report = inspect(busy)
        now = time.monotonic()
        if report.ok or now >= hard or (now >= deadline and not busy):
            break
    if report.ok:
        _all_there(report)
        return 0
    _describe(report)
    if report.busy:
        # Not a verdict that an image is missing: the time ran out first.
        say(f"k3s had not finished importing {_names(report.busy)} when the time ran out",
            err=True)
    say(f"still missing after the re-import: {', '.join(report.names()) or 'containerd'}", err=True)
    return 1


def cmd_unpack(args: argparse.Namespace) -> int:
    start = time.monotonic()
    hard = start + max(args.max_time, 0.0)
    if wait_for_import(min(start + args.wait, hard), hard):
        say("unpacked nothing: k3s was still importing when the time ran out", err=True)
        return 1
    picks = to_unpack()
    targets = image_targets() if picks else {}
    if picks is None or targets is None:
        say("unpacked nothing: containerd did not answer", err=True)
        return 1
    if not picks:
        say("nothing to unpack: every image this node runs is unpacked")
        return 0
    rate = args.rate * MIB
    pace = f"at most {args.rate:g} MiB/s" if rate > 0 else "unpaced"
    say(f"unpacking {len(picks)} image(s), {pace}: {', '.join(picks)}")
    began, total, failed = time.monotonic(), 0, []
    for i, ref in enumerate(picks):
        now, disk = time.monotonic(), disk_usage()
        if now >= hard:
            stop = f"out of time after {now - start:.0f}s"
        elif disk is not None and disk[1] < MIN_FREE_SHARE:
            stop = f"/var is {100 - 100 * disk[1]:.0f}% full"
        else:
            stop = ""
        if stop:
            failed += picks[i:]
            say(f"{stop}; leaving {len(picks) - i} image(s) to the kubelet", err=True)
            break
        target = targets.get(ref)
        if not target:
            say(f"could not unpack {ref}: containerd lists no digest for it", err=True)
            failed.append(ref)
            continue
        # Once more if it failed, after containerd answers again: a k3s
        # restart takes containerd down with it.
        if not (unpack_image(ref, target, hard)
                or (wait_for_containerd(hard) and unpack_image(ref, target, hard))):
            failed.append(ref)
            continue
        took, after = time.monotonic() - now, disk_usage()
        wrote = max(after[0] - disk[0], 0) if disk and after else 0
        total += wrote
        say(f"unpacked {ref}: {wrote / MIB:.0f} MiB in {took:.1f}s")
        # No faster than ``rate`` on average, counting what /var gained.
        pause = wrote / rate - took if rate > 0 else 0.0
        if pause > 0 and i + 1 < len(picks):
            _sleep(min(pause, max(hard - time.monotonic(), 0.0)))
    if failed:
        say(f"unpacked {len(picks) - len(failed)} of {len(picks)} image(s); the kubelet unpacks "
            f"the rest when it starts them: {', '.join(failed)}", err=True)
        return 1
    say(f"unpacked {len(picks)} image(s), {total / MIB:.0f} MiB, in {time.monotonic() - began:.0f}s")
    return 0


def main(argv: list[str] | None = None) -> int:
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(
        prog="spatium-k3s-images",
        description="Keep k3s's import of the slot's baked images honest (#1630).",
    )
    sub = parser.add_subparsers(dest="cmd", required=True)
    clear = sub.add_parser("clear-ingests", help="remove containerd's unfinished content writes")
    clear.add_argument("--min-idle", type=float, default=0.0,
                       help="only those idle this many seconds (default: all; k3s is not running)")
    clear.set_defaults(fn=cmd_clear_ingests)
    verify = sub.add_parser("verify", help="every baked tarball imported, every image present")
    verify.add_argument("--repair", action="store_true",
                        help="clear idle ingests and have k3s re-import what it missed")
    verify.add_argument("--wait", type=float, default=120.0,
                        help="seconds to wait for the kubelet while k3s has no tarball open")
    verify.add_argument("--repair-wait", type=float, default=180.0,
                        help="seconds to wait for k3s to re-import, once it has no tarball open")
    verify.add_argument("--max-wait", type=float, default=480.0,
                        help="seconds this check may take in all, even while k3s is still "
                             "importing (firstboot passes what is left of its start timeout)")
    verify.set_defaults(fn=cmd_verify)
    unpack = sub.add_parser("unpack", help="unpack the images this node will run before the "
                                           "kubelet needs them (#2788)")
    unpack.add_argument("--wait", type=float, default=120.0,
                        help="seconds to wait for the kubelet while k3s has no tarball open")
    unpack.add_argument("--max-time", type=float, default=1200.0,
                        help="seconds this may take in all, the wait for k3s's import included "
                             "(the kubelet unpacks whatever is left)")
    unpack.add_argument("--rate", type=float, default=UNPACK_RATE_MIB_S,
                        help="MiB/s the unpack writes at on average (default: the upgrade's own "
                             "rate, #2090; 0: unpaced)")
    unpack.set_defaults(fn=cmd_unpack)
    args = parser.parse_args(argv)
    return args.fn(args)


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
